he x-axis over the interval [ 5⇡ , 7⇡ ], where 6 6 x is a distance in meters from zero. If the density of the rod is given by the function ⇢(x) = 2 sin(x) + 6, in kilograms per meter, what is the mass of the rod in kilograms? ## Page 2 Definition 17. The work done by a variable force F along a line from x = a to x = b in the direction of the force is: b W = F (x) dx (in Joules, J ) a Z Observe: We are abstracting “area under the curve” on [a, b], to measure work . Example 105. Work from force. Set up an integral to measure the work required to move an object from x = 1 to x = 3 (in meters) in the presence of a force (in Newtons, N) 2 given by F (x) = , acting along the x-axis. The units for work are Joules, J = N m. x2 · Example 106. A spring in its equilibrium (on the x-axis) has a force F (x) = 100x applied to it. Set up an integral to measure how much work (in Joules, J) is done to compress the spring 0.5m. ## Page 3 Example 107. Suppose 20 N of force is required to stretch a spring 0.1 m from its equilib- rium. 1. Assuming the spring obeys Hooke’s Law, find the function for force. Definition 18. Hooke’s Law: Force required to keep a spring in position x units from equilibrium is: F (x) = k x. · 2. Set up an integral to measure the amount of work needed to stretch the spring from x = 0 to x = 0.25. 3. Set up an integral to measure the amount additional work required to stretch the spring from x = 0.25 to x = 3 from the equilibrium. Example 108. A spring requires 300 J to stretch from 2 m to 8 m. Assuming the spring obeys Hooke’s law, find the spring constant k. Then determine the function that models the force required to stretch/compress the spring from equilibrium. ## Page 4 Section 2.5b: Physical Applications - Pumping, Hydro- static Force Definition 19. Work = (Force) (Distance) · Vertical Pumping: “Split into pieces” W = F D = ⇢ V g y = k k k k k · · · · ) ⇣ ⌘ Example 109. For a given conical tank, the radius is 7 m and the height is 10 m. Acceler- ation due to gravity is 9.8 m , and the density of water is 1000 kg . sec2 m3 1. Set up the integral needed to determine how much work is required to pump all of the water over the top edge of the tank. 2. Set up the integral needed to determine how much work is required to pump the top 4 m of water out over the top edge of the tank. Reflect on modifications needed in the integral set up. ## Page 5 Example 110. For a given olive oil storage tank with triangular ends, the width is 14 m, the height is 10 m, and the length is 15 m. The tank is filled to a depth of 8 m. Acceleration due to gravity is 9.8 m , and the density of olive oil is 900 kg . sec2 m3 1. Set up the integral needed to determine how much work is required to pump the top 4 m of olive oil out through a standpipe that is 3 m above the top of the tank. Reflect on the modifications (due to geometry) needed in the integral set up from the previous example. 2. Set up the integral needed to determine how much work is required to pump the olive oil out through a standpipe that is 3 m above the top of the tank until 1 m depth of olive oil remains. Reflect on changes needed in the integral structure. ## Page 6 Example 111. A spherical tank with a radius of 8 m is completely full of sea water. The sea water is being pumped out the the very top of the tank. Acceleration due to gravity is 9.8 m , and the density of (salty) sea water is 1030 kg . Set up the integral needed to sec2 m3 determine how much work is required to pump all the sea water out through the very top. Discuss strategies for evaluating this integral. Example 112. Pumping gasoline out of a cylindrical tank on its side with a length of 10 m and a radius of 5 m half full of gasoline. Discuss the integral set up for the work require to empty the tank through an outlet pipe at the top of the tank. Density of gasoline is ⇢ = 737 kg/m3. ## Page 7 Example 113. Set up an integral to measure the work needed to pump all the water out of a cylindrical tank with heigh 